  <section id="shared">
    <h2>What it shares with the eSyria article</h2>
    <p>
      These passages match eSyria, almost word for word: the opening line (“one
      of the most important Syrian Circassian figures… first head of the
      Quneitra municipality in 1937”), the education section (Anbar school,
      Sorbonne, two diplomas), the Paris society, the 1930 election, the
      Literary Society of 4 March 1933, the school (1933–1943), the branches of
      17 May 1938, the 1937 Golan Trading Company and “Decree 262”,
      <em>Marj</em>, the peacemaking, the languages, the works, the lost
      manuscript, and his death in May 1952 and burial in the Qabartay quarter.
      The blog keeps eSyria’s “Qabartay says…” in the middle of the text, but
      never names Qabartay at the start, or eSyria at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>not independent.</strong> The blog is later (2016) than
        eSyria (2011) and repeats it, so it doesn’t confirm anything in it.
        Every review item on the eSyria page applies here too.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="new">
    <h2>What is new in this post</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A different birth year.</strong> “Born in the town of
        al-Mansura in the Syrian Golan in <strong>1903</strong>*”, with an
        asterisk that is never explained. The father, Ayyub Samkoug, is called a
        martyr who died fighting the Italian occupation of Libya in Tripoli in
        1911.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A political stand.</strong> In the opening he is called a writer
        and “nationalist” Circassian man of letters, and a “great politician”
        with national and patriotic positions in the Syrian parliament “in
        1931”. He is said to have opposed groups of Circassian youth joining the
        French forces and the formation of the Circassian military “squadrons”
        (<span lang="ar" dir="rtl">الكوكبات</span>), and to have been among the
        first to call for Syrian independence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Years in Paris.</strong> About ten years there “between 1921 and
        1929”, studying for his degree and working in a big Paris store to pay
        his way. He used the time to search French libraries for books and
        manuscripts on Circassian history.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A quotation from a writer.</strong> The poet and writer Abd
        al-Mu’in al-Maluhi
        (<span lang="ar" dir="rtl">عبد المعين الملوحي</span>), with an unexplained
        asterisk, is quoted as saying that Samkough was among the first to
        condemn Circassian volunteering in the French army, and the squadrons in
        particular, as an offence to “the owners of the house”, meaning the
        Syrian people who had given the Circassians hospitality.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A friend’s tribute.</strong> An unnamed friend recalls his
        graceful dancing, sweet playing and singing, his football team’s
        victories, his modesty, and how he stood by people in hard moments with
        advice, understanding and generosity with his “spirit and money”. It
        ends with a “flame extinguished in 1952” and his last words, “Long live
        my nation”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A reader’s comment</strong> (21 February 2016, signed “shojan”).
        The commenter objects to the point about the French army: Adyghe youth
        joined for a living, as thousands of Arab youths also did, many of whom
        later moved into the Syrian army and police. Circassian soldiers, he
        adds, fought well in the Palestine war, and people should be careful
        before accusing Circassians of collaboration with France.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
