  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>1903 or 1900.</strong> This post says 1903, while
          the very same eSyria text says 1900, which is what the Turkish
          profiles, Mirzoev and the dictionary entry give. 1903 matches the
          note on his portrait (the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/biography/">biography page</a> gives
          1900–1953). The asterisk after the year suggests a note
          that was lost when the text was copied.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>parliament in 1931 or 1930.</strong> The opening says
          1931 (as Mirzoev does). The body, copied from eSyria, says 1930.
          The post contradicts itself.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the arithmetic in Paris.</strong> “About ten years”
          between 1921 and 1929 is eight. It may be loose rounding, or two
          sources have been merged.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>a gap with other sources.</strong> If he was in Paris
          until 1929, he could not have been chairman of the Quneitra
          municipality from 1928 (Mirzoev), or have helped found <em>Marj</em>
          in 1927. Either the Paris dates or those dates are wrong. The post
          itself has the parliament seat in 1930 and no mayoralty until 1937.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the French.</strong> This post presents him as an
          opponent of Circassians serving the French and an early advocate of
          Syrian independence. Mirzoev says he <em>cooperated</em> with the
          French administration, and that the school opened with French support.
          The society also asked the French for autonomy for the Quneitra
          Circassians, and Kushkhabiev describes his group being accused of
          collaboration. These accounts are hard to reconcile. The only
          support given for the stand in this post is the quoted Maluhi
          passage.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the Maluhi quotation.</strong> It carries an
          unexplained asterisk, and gives no title, date or page. I can’t tell
          whether it is a real quotation, or when and where it was written.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>“squadrons”.</strong> I translated
          <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">الكوكبات</span> as “squadrons”, which I take to
          mean the Circassian cavalry units in the French forces in Syria.
          Literally the word means “groups” or “constellations”, so please check.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the friend’s tribute and “last words”.</strong> The
          friend isn’t named, and the passage is praise. The “last words” (“Long
          live my nation”) can’t be checked. Mirzoev mentions two football teams
          among the school’s activities, and the images page here has a football
          photo, but the tribute doesn’t say that his team was the school’s.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the comment.</strong> It is one anonymous reader’s
          opinion. It does give a plausible context for why young Circassians
          joined the French forces, but it isn’t evidence about Samkough.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>text errors.</strong> There is a stray closing quote
          after the education passage, and the line break in the middle of the
          Marj quotation is probably a paste slip. They suggest that the text
          was copied by hand or by machine.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
